[
    {
        "content": "<p>Here's <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=IsPGroup#doc\">docs#IsPGroup</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Group</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- A p-group is a group in which the order of every element is a power of `p`. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsPGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>It bothers me that the docstring is lying. For example, it's easy to show that <code>ZMod 6</code> is a <code>PGroup</code>, when interpreted multiplicatively, and that there is an element with order 3:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">GroupTheory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">PGroup</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsPGroup</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- \"A p-group is a group in which the order of every element is a power of `p`.\"</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsPGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Multiplicative</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ZMod</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">exists</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">ext</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">pow_one</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">toAdd_one</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">toAdd_pow</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">nsmul_eq_mul</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">cast_ofNat</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">conv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lhs</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lhs</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">change</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">zero_mul</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Multiplicative</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ZMod</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"o\">),</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">orderOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">two</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ZMod</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">exists</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Additive</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toMul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">two</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">addOrderOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">two</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">addOrderOf_eq_iff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">norm_num</span><span class=\"o\">)]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">And</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hmu</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hml</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">interval_cases</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">decide</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">decide</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">conv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lhs</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">arg</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">change</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Multiplicative</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ofAdd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">two</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">orderOf_ofAdd_eq_addOrderOf</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I'm guessing that the thinking behind the docstring is that p-groups are really only defined for prime <code>p</code>, in which case the docstring would be accurate. Should something about this setup be changed?</p>",
        "id": 629072990,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael",
        "timestamp": 1791144531
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43102\">#43102</a> switches the second <code>is</code> to <code>divides</code></p>",
        "id": 629076964,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1791146713
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That fixes the mismatch between the docstring and the Mathlib definition, but it doesn't seem to do much for the mismatch between the Mathlib definition and the mathematical definition -- or, for that matter, the rest of the documentation heading the <code>PGroup.lean</code> file.</p>",
        "id": 629077834,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael",
        "timestamp": 1791147273
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>mathematically I think p-groups are only defined for p prime, and in the case of p prime they are the same</p>",
        "id": 629077918,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1791147327
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If you defined <code>IsPGroup p G</code> as <code>∀ g : G, ∃ k : ℕ, orderOf g = p ^ k</code> it would match the docstring (directly) and (indirectly) the mathematical definition of a p-group, and a theorem would be theoretically available showing that <code>IsPGroup p G</code> proves <code>p.Prime</code>. <span aria-label=\"upside down\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f643\" role=\"img\" title=\"upside down\">:upside_down:</span></p>",
        "id": 629078800,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael",
        "timestamp": 1791147786
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Well not really, it's called a π-group.<br>\nSee <a href=\"https://mathoverflow.net/questions/476995/finite-simple-pi-groups\">https://mathoverflow.net/questions/476995/finite-simple-pi-groups</a>, and <a href=\"https://github.com/math-comp/math-comp/blob/9931c887da3d50ffb2a2e1b2e33b52ec9178a755/solvable/pgroup.v#L93\">Rocq's math-comp</a>.<br>\nWe could rename this to <code>IsPiGroup</code> and define an <code>abbrev IsPGroup</code> that requires <code>[Fact p.Prime]</code>, but that seems a bit silly to me. But expanding the docstring with this info seems nice.</p>\n<p>Also worth mentioning that only the prime factors matter and not their multiplicities (<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=isPGroup_iff_isPGroup_prod_primeFactors#doc\">docs#isPGroup_iff_isPGroup_prod_primeFactors</a>). We could define it with <code>Finset Nat.Primes</code> but again this seems like unnecessary friction.<br>\nIt's the same for <code>Sylow</code> btw (<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Sylow.equivProdPrimeFactors#doc\">docs#Sylow.equivProdPrimeFactors</a>)</p>",
        "id": 629079114,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1791147959
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The P-group in mathlib is recently generalized, it was required that <code>[Fact p.Prime]</code> before. I think there is a thread discussed before, where I proposed to use S-group instead of P-group, where S is a set of prime factors. But P-group is also understandable, isn't it?</p>\n<p>Also IMHO I don't agree <code>IsPiGroup</code> in mathlib, as <code>Pi</code> in Lean has special meaning (i.e. dependent map).</p>",
        "id": 629473620,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1791277094
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"366779\">Jz Pan</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/IsPGroup.20and.20its.20docstring/near/629473620\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The P-group in mathlib is recently generalized, it was required that <code>[Fact p.Prime]</code> before.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>AFAICT it was <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib3/pull/9082\">introduced</a> 5 years ago and it never required <code>[Fact p.Prime]</code>.</p>",
        "id": 629479513,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1791278292
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh sorry, I mean <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=CommGroup.primaryComponent#doc\">docs#CommGroup.primaryComponent</a> was generalized (it was required that <code>[Fact p.Prime]</code> before).</p>",
        "id": 629573511,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1791298858
    }
]